Sure! To sort your `myItemList` so that all items with `status === 'urgent'` appear first, and then the rest are sorted alphabetically by `name` in ascending order, you can use the `prioritizeAndSort` helper.

First, make sure you have the list ready (e.g., an array of objects like this):

```js
const myItemList = [
  { name: 'Alpha', status: 'normal' },
  { name: 'Bravo', status: 'urgent' },
  { name: 'Charlie', status: 'normal' },
  { name: 'Delta', status: 'urgent' }
];
```

Now, call the function:

```js
const sorted = prioritizeAndSort({
  items: JSON.stringify(myItemList),   // Pass the array as a JSON string
  priorityStatus: 'urgent',            // Items with this status go first
  ascending: 'true'                    // Sort the non‑urgent items ascendingly
});

console.log(sorted);
```

The `sorted` variable will contain the array sorted as requested. If you run this with the example above, the output will be:

```js
[
  { name: 'Bravo', status: 'urgent' },
  { name: 'Delta', status: 'urgent' },
  { name: 'Alpha', status: 'normal' },
  { name: 'Charlie', status: 'normal' }
]
```

Feel free to replace `myItemList` with your actual data. Let me know if you’d like help integrating this into your project or if you encounter any issues!
